Distributions are powerful generalizations of functions which arise often when solving physical equations. We start with the intuitive example of the Dirac delta and return to a more general discussions of distributions. 

\section{The Dirac Delta}

Informally defined, the Dirac delta is a function which satisfies the following equation.
\begin{equation}
\delta(x) = \begin{cases}
+\infty, & x = 0 \\
0, & x \neq 0 \\
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
This "function" $\delta$ is also constrained to obey the following equation.
\begin{equation}
    \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \delta(x) dx = 1
\end{equation}
The issue here of course is that there exists no such function on $\mathbb{R}$ since functions can't take the value of $\infty$. The Dirac delta is thus an example of a \textit{distribution}, a type of generalized function. Before we provide formal definitions, it will be useful to create a little more structured definition of the Dirac delta. Let's first start with introducing a simple integral we will find useful

\begin{equation}
d_K(x) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-K/2}^{K/2} e^{ikx} dk
\end{equation}

How can we solve this complex integral? Recall the formula

\[ e^{ikx} = \cos(kx) + i \sin(kx) \]

and rewrite
\begin{align}
    d_K(x) &= \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-K/2}^{K/2} e^{ikx} dk \\
    &= \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-K/2}^{K/2} \cos(kx) + i \sin(kx) dk \\
    &= \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-K/2}^{K/2} \cos(kx)  dk + \frac{i}{2\pi} \int_{-K/2}^{K/2} \sin(kx) dk \\
    &= \frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{\sin(kx)}{kx} \Big|^{K/2}_{-K/2} - \frac{i}{2\pi}  \frac{\cos(kx)}{kx} \Big | _{-K/2}^{K/2} \\
\end{align}

To evaluate the last expression, note that $\cos$ is an even function and that $\sin$ is an odd function so we gain

\begin{align}
    d_K(x) &= \frac{1}{\pi x} \sin{\frac{Kx}{2}}
\end{align}

These functions spike at the origin with the spike growing more pronounced as $K \to \infty$ as the plot below shows

\begin{figure}
    \centering    \includegraphics{figures/Mathematical Foundations/distributions/distributions_d_50.png}
    \caption{Plot of $d_{50}$.}
    \label{fig:d_K}
\end{figure}

$d_K(x)$ is damped by the factor $\frac{1}{\pi x}$ the further you get from the origin. Note that formally, $d_K(0)$ is undefined, but we can evaluate a reasonable value at the origin, by using L'H\^{o}pital's rule

\begin{align}
    d_K(0) &:= \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sin{\tfrac{Kx}{2}}}{\pi x} \\
    &= \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\tfrac{K}{2}\cos{\tfrac{Kx}{2}}}{\pi} \\
    &= \frac{K}{2\pi}
\end{align}

The definite integral of $d_K(x)$ over the real line equals $1$ for all $K$. To show this, consider the following definite integral

\begin{align}
    \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} d_K(x) dx &= \int_{\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\pi x} \sin \tfrac{Kx}{2}  dx\\
    &= \frac{1}{\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\sin{\tfrac{Kx}{2}}}{x} dx \\
\end{align}
Let's perform the substitution $y=\frac{Kx}{2}$. Then $x = \frac{2y}{K}$
\begin{align}
    \frac{1}{\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\sin{\tfrac{Kx}{2}}}{x} dx
    &= \frac{1}{\pi}\frac{2}{K} \frac{K}{2} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\sin{y}}{y} dy \\
    &= \frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\infty} ^ {\infty} \frac{\sin(y)}{y} dy \\
    &= \frac{1}{\pi}\pi \\
    &= 1
\end{align}

